  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nSkies were partly to mostly cloudy across the region this\nafternoon with temperatures in the 70s. Isolated to scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms developed in response to 850mb warm\nadvection ahead of a weak cold front across northwest and west-\ncentral WI. Some of this activity will make it into central and \nnorth-central WI towards 00z Wednesday. Meanwhile, a cold front \nacross Minnesota will trigger additional shower and thunderstorm \nactivity that should move into central and north-central WI during\nthe mid to late evening hours, then across northeast WI after \n05z. Some of the stronger storms across central and north-central \nWI could be strong or severe with damaging wind gusts to 60 mph, \nhail up to one inch in diameter and brief heavy rain. Bufkit \nsoundings indicated 500 to 1,000 J/KG and decent shear values \naround 30 knots. The Storm Prediction Center Day 1 Outlook has a \nMarginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) west of a Wisconsin Rapids to Land O'\nLakes line. \n\nThere will be a break in the showers and storms overnight.\nMeanwhile, an upper trough will swing across the area on \nWednesday. Daytime heating combined with enough CAPE would support\nscattered thunderstorms over the northeast portion of the state. \nSome of the storms will be capable of producing small hail, gusty \nwinds to 45 mph and brief heavy rain. Inverted V sounding would \nsupport gusty winds. \n\nDry conditions expected Wednesday night through Friday as high\npressure moves across the region. The next chance of storms\narrives Friday night into Saturday with chances of storms\nlingering through the weekend. There is the possibility of a \nfew thunderstorm complexes over the weekend, but the details\non location and timing are uncertain at this time. Dry conditions\nreturn Monday and Tuesday. High temperatures during the period\nshould be at or slightly above normal.",
